      "summary": "The global expansion of physical AI has made securing resilient domestic supply chains for robots, drones and other moving \"things\" a top priority China dominates the global supply chain in these areas, in addition to key materials like semiconductor wafers and batteries The United States, India, Japan, South Korea and European countries are investing heavily in localized production to build sovereign supply chains",
      "raw_text": "The global expansion of physical AI has made securing resilient domestic supply chains for robots, drones and other moving \"things\" a top priority. China dominates the global supply chain in these areas, in addition to key materials like semiconductor wafers and batteries. The United States, India, Japan, South Korea and European countries are investing heavily in localized production to build sovereign supply chains. Onshoring presents severe cost and expertise hurdles. To overcome design gaps, companies like Monumo and Neural Concept deploy advanced physics-based AI platforms that optimize designs and automate engineering workflows to rebuild local design competencies. Firms like Gl\u012bd are constructing entirely U.S.-sourced dual mode, autonomous logistics platforms to strengthen the domestic industrial base.\n...\nPhysical AI and Autonomy of Things (**AoT\u00ae**)implementations with drones (airborne, underwater, ocean), robots and autonomous vehicles needs a secure chain for components -\n\n---\n\nTitle: Autonomous Space Cargo Network (ASCN): AI-Driven Logistics Automation and Digital Twin Integration for Space Mobility\nURL: https://console.sweetspotgov.com/sbirs/91289ada-f32c-521c-b81c-833c59b8f4e1\nPublished: 2026-04-13T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThe U.S. Air Force is seeking an SBIR solution for the Autonomous Space Cargo Network to modernize cargo handling and logistics automation for the Space Joint Movement Complex and the broader Department of War space mobility enterprise. The work centers on an Autonomous Mobile Robot-based platform that combines autonomous cargo transport, AI-driven logistics decision support, and digital twin integration to improve throughput, adaptability, and resilience. Phase I calls for a conceptual design, early prototype, feasibility assessment, and integration planning, while Phase II advances to a fully operational prototype with testing, performance metrics, and interoperability with DoW and commercial logistics systems. The effort emphasizes cybersecure architecture, real-time cargo movement, and compatibility with systems such as USTRANSCOM, SSC, DLA, and the Spaceport of the Future COP logistics module. Applications are due June 3, 2026 at 16:00 UTC under solicitation DAF26BZ01-NV500 / 26.BZ.\n...\nThe DAF26BZ01-NV500 Autonomous Space Cargo Network (ASCN) initiative seeks to develop an AI-driven logistics automation platform using Autonomous Mobile Robots (AMRs) to modernize cargo handling at the Space Joint Movement Complex (SJMC) and across the Department of War's (DoW) space mobility enterprise. This effort aims to enhance cargo transport, real-time movement, and resilient logistics in contested environments through autonomous robotics, intelligent decision-support, and digital twin technology. Key capabilities include autonomous cargo handling, AI-driven command and control, machine learning for mission adaptability, interoperability with commercial and military logistics, and a cybersecure robotics architecture. Phase I focuses on conceptual design and prototyping AMR-based systems, while Phase II involves developing and demonstrating a fully operational prototype. Phase III outlines dual-use applications for military logistics and commercial space sectors, with anticipated Technology Readiness Levels progressing from 3-4 in Phase I to 7-9 in Phase III.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Autonomy, Robotics, and Predictive Analytics: Sustainment's ...\nURL: https://mwi.westpoint.edu/autonomy-robotics-and-predictive-analytics-sustainments-technology-trifecta-and-the-future-of-war/\nPublished: 2026-05-05T07:05:37.000Z\nAuthor: Charlie Phelps\nHighlights:\nTo fight and win in large-scale combat operations, the Army can no longer rely on the static, centralized, and relatively secure logistics models of the past two decades. Victory will demand a fundamental revolution in sustainment, as depicted in the fictional scenario above, moving from a reactive to a proactive and resilient enterprise. This transformation hinges on the aggressive integration of a technological trinity: autonomous systems, advanced robotics, and AI-powered predictive analytics. By embracing this trifecta, the Army can build a logistics network that is not only efficient but also survivable, agile, and intelligent enough to sustain the force at the speed of modern warfare.\n...\nHowever, the success of multidomain operations is inextricably linked to the feasibility of its sustainment. Without a resilient and responsive logistics network, maneuver forces risk culmination, and the entire operational concept is placed in jeopardy. The solution lies in transforming the logistics enterprise into one that can deliver what Lieutenant General Christopher O. Mohan calls \u201c predictive logistics,\u201d a proactive, data-driven approach that ensures the right resources are in the right place at the right time. This vision can only be realized through the deliberate integration of autonomous systems, robotics, and predictive analytics.\n\nIn a co",
